  "title": "Day 2: Section 2.1 - Rates of Change, Secant and Tangent Lines",
  "body": " Day 2: Section 2.1 - Rates of Change, Secant and Tangent Lines   Today we want to start thinking about rates of change one of the most foundational ideas in all of Calculus. We'll start by reviewing how to calculate rates of change, what physical quantities they represent, and how to visualize them on a graph.  More importantly, we want to build an intuition for what these calculations actually mean. So many real-world situations can be modeled by functions. If we can develop better tools to study the math, we can solve much more complex problems in the real world. Let's look at the following example.     Checkpoint  The following table shows the position (in meters) of a cyclist as a function of time (in seconds).    (seconds)  0  1  2  3  4  5    (meters)  0  1.5  5.1  10.5  17.7  25.8      Find the average velocity over the time period . Include units in your answer. Recall that average velocity is given by the change in position divided by the elapsed time.    Suppose you'd like to know the cyclist's instantaneous velocity at the moment . Can we compute the average velocity over the time period ? Why or why not?    We can use average velocities over appropriate intervals to approximate an instantaneous velocity. What are some specific intervals near that would help estimate the instantaneous velocity at ?      Key Details  Let's write down a few key details to help us synthesize our approach to the problem above.    The average rate of change (AROC) of over the interval is given by     We can not find an rate of change by using the formula for the average rate of change.    We can find an average rate of change over a small interval this gives us an of the instantaneous rate of change.        Secant Lines and Average Rate of Change  Let's think about what average and instantaneous velocities look like on a graph. Here's a generic graph of our situation:    Generic curve showing two points connected by a secant line illustrating average rate of change.      Graphically, when we calculate average velocity over a time interval, we're really finding the average rate of change as we move from on the function to .    Let's look at the slope of the line between those points:       Remember that a line connecting two points on a function is called a line.  We've really discovered an important relationship here:    Fact  The of a secant line is the  of the function over the interval defined by our points.  In our example above, we were looking at the average rate of change of position, which is just another way of saying average velocity. It's a nice example!  We'll do a lot with the formula associated with this idea, so let's write it down:    Formula  The average rate of change (AROC) of with respect to over the interval is   Notes:    This fraction (in either form) is often referred to as the .    We can get the second form of the difference quotient by making the substitution  (note that we need to be sure ).      Definition  The last thing we need to complete our picture of mathematically representing rates of change is the idea of a tangent line .  The tangent line to at is the line that the graph of at the point .  We care about tangent lines because:    As approaches , the secant line becomes closer to the tangent line at (see GeoGebra ).    That is, the of a tangent line to a function at represents the function's  at .        Checkpoint (Think - Pair - Share)  Consider the parabola . Use sketches to order the following quantities from smallest to largest:     The slope of the secant line between and  The slope of the tangent line at  The instantaneous rate of change of at     Answer:          Hint: Recall how the slope is related to the steepness and direction of a line (see GeoGebra ).    Graph of a downward-opening parabola f(x) with vertex at x = 2.    The study of secant and tangent lines, and how they measure rates of change, is what motivates a lot of Calculus learning this framework is a great start!    Checkpoint (Exercise)  Suppose you take 2 hours to cross a 1 mile long bridge. How fast do you have to return if you want your average round-trip velocity to be 1 mph?    "
